From 6fa810bec5908ae3e3dea59d983d5d0f3efaf174 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:01:07 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 50/53] Renderer: presents on a thread of their own

With frame interpolation the presents between flips were made on the
emulation thread, in the kernel's idle time or wherever the game's code let
them; patch 0048 moved them into the idle time at the cost of 9 ms more delay.
Now a thread of the renderer's own makes every present at its due time while
the game's code runs: interpolation's (the replay of the older frame with
blended transforms), a frame at its flip without interpolation (thinned to the
display's refresh), and the interface's while the game is paused, which the
emulation thread waits for as it waited for its own present.

The emulation thread records and submits each game frame as before. The
renderer's state is behind one lock, which the emulation thread holds for each
call into the renderer and lets a waiting present thread take between calls;
the present thread holds it while it records and submits a present. The
swapchain is the present thread's: it acquires (up to 100 ms, reporting a
display that takes no image) and presents without the renderer's lock, so a
display that keeps its images never holds the game. Every use of the queue,
ImGui's and the texture pack's uploads included, is behind a queue lock. The
interface's frame is built holding the renderer, so a present never draws one
half built. The present clock counts a frame's work to its flip only (19.6 ms
of delay on the PC instead of 29); patch 0048's idle-time logic and code poll
do nothing while the thread runs. The thread waits on a mailbox a flip wakes
and sleeps the last stretch on a high-resolution timer. The program stops it
when the game has stopped (stop_presenting), an exit handler on any other exit,
and shutdown() before anything else.

<prefix>_NO_PRESENT_THREAD presents on the emulation thread exactly as patch
0048 did; <prefix>_PRESENT_AFTER_FLIP there with the pacing before it. Android
keeps the emulation thread's presents. Test switches: <prefix>_TEST_GUEST_SPIN_MS
(a slower CPU's game code after each flip) and the input script's window steps.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
